      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0">
        Say the asymmetry out loud rather than softening it:
        <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">the shared sheet is generated and gated; each artboard’s own file is invisible.</strong>
        The blocker is <em>reach</em>, not rules — the rules exist, and a gate would need either the
        artboards inside the repo or an authenticated client to apply them. "We should gate it" and
        "we cannot see it" need different work, and conflating them is how a room ends up believing
        artboards are checked when they are not.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 — Proof -->
    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="proof" class="docs-section-title">What it found here</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1rem">
        On 2026-08-26 and 27 this library was redesigned <em>through</em> the canvas.
        <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">31 artboards</strong> — 29 component sheets plus
        two studies — covering <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">29 of 29 components</strong>.
        The two studies were a typography exploration and a foundations sheet, and the foundations
        sheet was itself generated from <code>tokens.css</code>: even on the ungated surface, the
        parts that could be generated were.
      </p>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1rem">
        Read that coverage number the way the fence above tells you to. It comes from the
        hand-maintained registry, not from a measurement: a person wrote down which artboard
        covers which component, and <code>gen-design-status.mjs</code> counts the rows. Nothing
        opened an artboard to check. The <em>defects</em> below are hard — each one is a decision
        record with a diff behind it — but <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">29 of 29 is
        a claim, not a check</strong>, and on this surface that is the only kind of coverage
        number available. That is the fence, seen from the side where it costs you something.
      </p>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1.25rem">
        Direction matters for reading what follows. This was a deliberate redesign, so the code and
        the artboards were canonical and the Figma masters were the <em>target</em> of the transfer,
        stale by definition until rebuilt. What still counted as a defect was anything wrong on its
        own terms — internally inconsistent, invisible to users, or unbindable regardless of what
        Figma happened to hold. Seventeen decision records carry the redesign itself as their origin, <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">ADR-0041 through ADR-0057</strong>,
        thirteen of which name an artboard or its findings document as their source. Four are worth knowing by heart.
      </p>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem">
        {DEFECTS.map(d => (
          <div style="padding: 1rem 1.15rem; background: var(--ui-color-surface-raised); border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border); border-radius: var(--ui-radius-md)">
            <div style="display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.6rem; margin-bottom: 0.4rem; flex-wrap: wrap">
              <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ui-color-primary); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ui-color-primary) 10%, transparent); padding: 0.1rem 0.45rem; border-radius: 3px">{d.adr}</span>
              <span style="font-size: 0.92rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ui-color-text)">{d.title}</span>
            </div>
            <p style="margin: 0 0 0.6rem; font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.6">{d.detail}</p>
            <blockquote style="margin: 0 0 0.6rem; padding-left: 0.75rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--ui-color-primary); font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--ui-color-text); line-height: 1.6">{d.quote}</blockquote>
            <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.6"><strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">What it produced:</strong> {d.remedy}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">The mechanism, not the count</h3>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 0.75rem">
        Notice what the four have in common. None was found by scanning; each was found by having to
        <em>state</em> something on a sheet — a row height, an anatomy ruler, what the alert’s icon
        <em>is</em>. And the richest ones came from drawing a whole family on one canvas rather than
        one component in isolation. From the redesign’s own findings document, on the four
        choice-control sheets:
      </p>
      <blockquote style="margin: 0 0 1rem; padding-left: 0.9rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--ui-color-primary); color: var(--ui-color-text); line-height: 1.65; font-size: 0.9rem">
        four questions that only appear when the family is drawn together. None is a bug in one
        component; each is an inconsistency <em>between</em> components that every gate passes.
      </blockquote>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 0.75rem">
        That is the argument for the whole track, and it does not contradict the fence — it completes
        it. A gate only checks the mechanism its author imagined; ADR-0050 says so in as many words,
        and ADR-0055 then found the next mechanism the author of ADR-0050 had not imagined. Cheap,
        divergent, ungated drawing is how you find the mechanism nobody imagined. The gate is how you
        keep it found.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Both halves</span>
        Drawing found the defect. The gate keeps it found. Neither one alone would have held.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 — Reviewing the tool's output -->
    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="review" class="docs-section-title">Reviewing what the tool produced</h2>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1rem">
        When <code>/design-sync</code> pushed this library into a claude.ai/design design system on
        <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">2026-08-26</strong>, the manifest it generated was
        checked line by line against the repo. Four defects were confirmed. Read this as a dated
        observation of one run, not as a present-tense property of the tool: the manifest lives in
        that external project, not on disk here, so this page cannot re-check it.
      </p>
      <table class="docs-props-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th style="width: 22%">Defect</th>
            <th style="width: 38%">What the manifest claimed</th>
            <th>How you catch it</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {SYNC_DEFECTS.map(row => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">{row.defect}</strong></td>
              <td>{row.claim}</td>
              <td>{row.caught}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 1rem 0 0.75rem">
        Each row is caught a different way, and that is the actual lesson: there is no single
        "check the AI output" move. You grep for existence, you read a type against its value, you
        compare a list against a declared public surface, and you read a rule’s configuration rather
        than its name.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Standing rule</span>
        The manifest is reference, never input. Derive from <code>tokens.css</code>.
      </div>
      <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; margin: 1rem 0 0">
        And the counterweight, from the same run: that same manifest’s three adherence rules — raw
        hex should become a token via <code>var()</code>, a raw px value should become a spacing
        token, <code>font-family</code> outside the design-system list is a violation — were judged
        reusable nearly verbatim. One tool produced both the bad metadata and the good rule set. The
        lesson is review, not distrust. <code>/design-sync</code> is also React-only, so for an
        Angular or Vue cohort it is a demo on someone else’s machine or it is cut.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 — Limits -->
